  "print": "bird",
  "_about": "DILOPHOSAURUS WETHERILLI (sculpt overlay, 2026-11-05, after Marsh & Rowe 2020): 7 m, ~400 kg, the hips 1.6 m up -- slender and lightly built; a long neck flexed so the head is held level; a 0.59 m skull with a narrow snout and the subnarial notch (the kink in the upper jaw between premaxilla and maxilla, below the nostril); two thin, tall, arched crests side by side along the skull roof from the nostril to over the eye, splayed a little; long arms with three working fingers (the first short with a big claw); long legs, three toes. Olive-grey with a darker back, faint darker saddle, pale belly; the crests rufous. (2026-11-08, review.py: claws) The claws RE-ROOTED (review.py): each a sheath as wide as the claw where it leaves the toe, sunk in it, the join faired (horn `fair`: grown out of the skin in its paint), darker at the base fading to its own colour at the point -- no pale cut edge where it leaves the fur. (2026-11-08, review.py: hand claws) The HAND: each finger one smooth horn (faired into the hand, a rounded fingertip) instead of a field cone and a six-sided tube that came out as blocky shards on the coarse grid round the claws' roots; the claws come out of the fingertips, 0.72 of their girth, along their old hooks (no fillet). (2026-11-08, plates.py) The twin CRESTS are thin `fin` sheets (they were two flat field ells each: a lumpy block on the skull), rooted along the skull top either side of the midline and leaning out 7 degrees: low at the nostril, rising in a convex arc to their highest over the front of the eye, ending behind in a small backward point; faired into the skull, the top edge banded in the rim colour. (2026-11-08, review.py: fingers) The FINGERS (review.py knuckle_fingers): slimmer, two knuckles swelling along each, the arm's scales on them, each running into its claw -- the claw's base nearly the fingertip's girth and round, a sheath the finger ends in (they read as smooth sausages with a thin claw poking out).",
